```{r libs}
library(spiDE)
library(SpatialExperiment)
library(ggplot2)
library(patchwork)
source(system.file("spide_palette.R", package = "spiDE"))
```

# Introduction

`spiDE` looks for **neighbourhood-dependent differential expression**: within an
index cell type, it asks whether a gene's response to an experimental condition
depends on the local density of surrounding cell types (the *niche*). Evaluating
a method like this honestly requires simulated data whose statistical structure
matches real spatial transcriptomics — otherwise a benchmark can flatter (or
unfairly penalise) the method.

This vignette has two parts. First we **look at a simulated dataset** and check
that it reproduces the hallmarks of real spatial data: very low, gene-specific
counts and a mean–variance relationship in which lowly expressed genes are the
most overdispersed. Second we summarise a **large benchmark study** of the spiDE
model — its false-positive control, its statistical power, and its robustness to
the spatial arrangement of cells.

The benchmark figures below are read from summary tables shipped with the
package (`inst/extdata/benchmark/`). They are produced by a self-contained
simulation study designed to run on a compute cluster; the code, and
instructions for regenerating the tables at full scale, live in the package's
`research/` directory.

# What a realistic spatial dataset looks like

Real spatial transcriptomics assays (imaging platforms such as Xenium and CosMx,
or sequencing platforms at single-cell resolution) share three features that a
naïve simulation misses:

1. **many genes** — a panel or transcriptome of ten thousand or more;
2. **very low counts** — most gene–cell measurements are 0, 1, 2 or occasionally
   3, with a small number of highly expressed genes;
3. **a mean–variance trend** — overdispersion is not constant; lowly expressed
   genes are relatively far more variable than highly expressed ones.

The `spiDE` simulator reproduces all three with a negative-binomial model whose
per-gene mean is drawn from a Gamma distribution and whose dispersion follows the
[splatter](https://bioconductor.org/packages/splatter/)-style trend
`bcv = bcv.common + bcv.disp / sqrt(mean)` (so the NB size, `1 / bcv²`, *rises*
with abundance). We draw one large dataset to inspect these properties.

```{r showcase-sim}
# a 12,000-gene dataset with a spatial niche gradient and one planted signal
n_genes <- 12000L
field   <- 500
set.seed(1)

sim_showcase <- function(n_per, condition) {
  x <- runif(n_per, 0, field)
  y <- runif(n_per, 0, field)
  # B cells enrich at high x -> a left-to-right niche gradient
  ct <- ifelse(x > 0.6 * field & runif(n_per) < 0.7, "B",
               sample(c("A", "C"), n_per, replace = TRUE))
  lib <- rlnorm(n_per, -0.02, 0.2)
  eff <- matrix(0, n_genes, n_per)
  if (condition == "Responder") {                 # plant G1 in A cells vs x
    eff[1, ] <- 2.5 * (ct == "A") * (x / field)
  }
  Y <- spiDE:::.simCounts(gp, ct, log_effect = eff, lib.size = lib)
  list(Y = Y, x = x, y = y, ct = ct, condition = condition)
}

gene_names <- sprintf("G%d", seq_len(n_genes))
# total.count sets an imaging-ST-like sequencing depth (~1500-2000 counts/cell)
gp <- spiDE:::.simGeneParams(gene_names, boost = "G1", boost.gmean = 10,
                             total.count = 1500)
resp    <- sim_showcase(3000, "Responder")
nonresp <- sim_showcase(3000, "Non-responder")
Y <- cbind(resp$Y, nonresp$Y)
```

## Counts are low and sparse

```{r showcase-counts, fig.height = 3.6, fig.cap = "Distribution of individual gene-cell counts. The overwhelming majority are 0, 1 or 2, as in real imaging-based spatial data."}
ct_tab <- table(factor(pmin(as.vector(Y), 6L), levels = 0:6))
cdf <- data.frame(count = names(ct_tab),
                  frac = as.numeric(ct_tab) / sum(ct_tab))
cdf$count <- factor(cdf$count, levels = 0:6,
                    labels = c(0:5, "6+"))
zero_pct <- round(100 * mean(Y == 0))
lib_med  <- round(median(colSums(Y)))

ggplot(cdf, aes(count, frac)) +
  geom_col(fill = spide_pal$accent, width = 0.75) +
  geom_text(aes(label = scales::percent(frac, accuracy = 1)), vjust = -0.4,
            size = 3) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent, expand = expansion(c(0, 0.1))) +
  labs(x = "count value", y = "fraction of gene-cell measurements",
       title = sprintf("Counts are dominated by zeros (%d%% zero)", zero_pct))
```

Aggregated across genes, each cell carries a realistic sequencing depth — here a
median of about `r format(lib_med, big.mark = ",")` total counts per cell — even
though almost every individual gene–cell measurement is zero or one.

## Overdispersion decreases with abundance

For each gene we estimate its mean expression and its biological coefficient of
variation (BCV) from the counts. Plotting BCV against mean abundance recovers the
characteristic **downward trend**: rare genes are the most overdispersed, and the
scatter follows the trend the simulator was built from.

```{r showcase-bcv, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Empirical mean-variance trend. Each point is a gene; the biological coefficient of variation falls as mean expression rises. The line is the model's true trend."}
gmean <- rowMeans(Y)
gvar  <- rowSums((Y - gmean)^2) / (ncol(Y) - 1)
# NB biological coefficient of variation, guarding the Poisson floor
bcv <- sqrt(pmax(gvar - gmean, 0) / gmean^2)
keep <- gmean > 0.02 & is.finite(bcv) & bcv > 0
trend <- data.frame(m = 10^seq(log10(0.05), log10(max(gmean)), length = 100))
trend$bcv <- 0.3 + 1 / sqrt(trend$m)          # bcv.common + bcv.disp / sqrt(mean)

ggplot(data.frame(gmean = gmean[keep], bcv = bcv[keep]), aes(gmean, bcv)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.15, size = 0.5, colour = spide_pal$accent) +
  geom_line(data = trend, aes(m, bcv), colour = spide_pal$combine[["Brown"]],
            linewidth = 1) +
  scale_x_log10() + scale_y_log10() +
  labs(x = "mean expression (log scale)", y = "biological CV (log scale)",
       title = "Lowly expressed genes are the most overdispersed")
```

## Genes and cell types differ in expression

Gene means span several orders of magnitude, and each cell type has its own
expression profile — the biological structure `spiDE` conditions on.

```{r showcase-genes, fig.height = 3.8, fig.cap = "Left: the distribution of per-gene mean expression is heavy-tailed, most genes very low. Right: mean expression differs systematically between cell types."}
p_dist <- ggplot(data.frame(gmean = gmean[gmean > 0]), aes(gmean)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 50, fill = spide_pal$accent) +
  scale_x_log10() +
  labs(x = "per-gene mean (log scale)", y = "number of genes",
       title = "Gene means vary widely")

ctmean <- data.frame(
  cell_type = c(resp$ct, nonresp$ct),
  total = colSums(Y))
p_ct <- ggplot(ctmean, aes(cell_type, total, fill = cell_type)) +
  geom_boxplot(outlier.size = 0.3, alpha = 0.9) +
  scale_fill_spide("celltype") +
  scale_y_log10() +
  labs(x = "cell type", y = "total counts per cell (log scale)",
       title = "Cell types differ") +
  theme(legend.position = "none")

p_dist + p_ct
```

## The spatial niche and the planted signal

The simulator places `B` cells so their density rises from left to right; this
gradient is what `buildNiches()` turns into a niche covariate. The planted
effect is a three-way interaction — gene `G1` is up-regulated **only** in the
index cell type `A`, **only** in Responder samples, and **in proportion to the
local `B` density**. It is visible as a left-to-right expression gradient that
appears in exactly one panel below.

```{r showcase-spatial, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Left: cell-type layout in the Responder sample; B cells concentrate at high x. Right: G1 expression in A cells only. The high-x gradient appears in the Responder but not the Non-responder -- the celltype:condition:niche effect spiDE tests for."}
layout_df <- data.frame(x = resp$x, y = resp$y, cell_type = resp$ct)
p_layout <- ggplot(layout_df, aes(x, y, colour = cell_type)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.6, alpha = 0.8) +
  scale_colour_spide("celltype") +
  coord_equal() +
  guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 2, alpha = 1))) +
  labs(title = "Cell-type layout (Responder)")

g1 <- rbind(
  data.frame(x = resp$x, y = resp$y, ct = resp$ct,
             G1 = log1p(resp$Y[1, ]), condition = "Responder"),
  data.frame(x = nonresp$x, y = nonresp$y, ct = nonresp$ct,
             G1 = log1p(nonresp$Y[1, ]), condition = "Non-responder"))
p_signal <- ggplot(subset(g1, ct == "A"), aes(x, y, colour = G1)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.7) +
  scale_colour_viridis_c(option = "magma") +
  facet_wrap(~ condition) +
  coord_equal() +
  labs(title = "G1 in A cells", colour = "log1p(G1)")

p_layout + p_signal + patchwork::plot_layout(widths = c(1, 1.6))
```

# Benchmarking the spiDE model

The remainder of this vignette summarises a benchmark study that fits `spiDE` to
many such datasets. The full study (see `research/`) sweeps the number of
patient samples, the number of cells per sample, the effect size and the spatial
layout, and repeats each design point over many random replicates. Here we load
its summary tables.

```{r load-bench}
bench_dir <- system.file("extdata", "benchmark", package = "spiDE")
bench <- function(f) {
  path <- file.path(bench_dir, f)
  if (!file.exists(path)) return(NULL)
  if (grepl("\\.rds$", f)) readRDS(path) else
    utils::read.csv(path, check.names = FALSE)
}
```

`spiDE` offers three inference modes, compared throughout:

* **fixed** (`random = "none"`) — treats every cell as independent;
* **intercept** (`random = "intercept"`) — a per-patient random intercept;
* **slope** (`random = "slope"`) — additionally random per-patient niche slopes.

The mixed-effects modes exist because a patient contributes many cells but only
one condition label, so cells are *pseudo-replicates*: treating them as
independent overstates the evidence for a condition effect. The benchmark
quantifies the consequences.

## False-positive control under the null

When no effect is planted, a well-calibrated test rejects the null at rate
$\alpha$. The figure shows the realised type-I error at $\alpha = 0.05$ as the
number of patient samples grows, for each mode and two spatial layouts.

```{r bench-type1, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Type-I error at alpha = 0.05 under the null. The dashed line is the nominal rate. The mixed-effects modes track it as samples accrue; the fixed-effects mode does not correct for pseudo-replication."}
t1 <- bench("null_type1.csv")
if (!is.null(t1)) {
  t1$method <- factor(t1$method, levels = names(spide_pal$method))
  ggplot(t1, aes(S, type1, colour = method)) +
    geom_hline(yintercept = 0.05, linetype = 2, colour = spide_pal$ref) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 2) +
    facet_wrap(~ layout) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = "number of patient samples (S)",
         y = "P(reject | null) at alpha = 0.05",
         title = "Null calibration versus sample size")
}
```

A p-value QQ plot makes the same point across the whole distribution rather than
at a single threshold: calibrated p-values lie on the diagonal.

```{r bench-qq, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "QQ plot of null response-niche p-values pooled over replicates. Points on the diagonal are calibrated; points above it are anti-conservative (too many small p-values)."}
pv <- bench("null_pvalues.rds")
if (!is.null(pv)) {
  Smax <- max(pv$S)
  pv <- pv[pv$S == Smax & pv$layout == "gradient", ]
  qq <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(pv$p, pv$method), function(p) {
    p <- sort(p[is.finite(p)])
    data.frame(method = NA, expected = -log10(ppoints(length(p))),
               observed = -log10(pmax(p, 1e-8)))
  }))
  qq$method <- rep(names(split(pv$p, pv$method)),
                   times = vapply(split(pv$p, pv$method),
                                  function(p) length(p[is.finite(p)]), 1L))
  qq$method <- factor(qq$method, levels = names(spide_pal$method))
  ggplot(qq, aes(expected, observed, colour = method)) +
    geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2, colour = spide_pal$ref) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = expression(-log[10]("expected p")),
         y = expression(-log[10]("observed p")),
         title = sprintf("Null p-value calibration (S = %d)", Smax))
}
```

## Power: samples buy it, cells do not

With a real effect planted, we measure **power** — the fraction of truly
responding genes detected. The two panels are the central result: power climbs
with the number of patient **samples** but is essentially flat in the number of
**cells per sample**. Because the condition label is shared by all cells of a
patient, extra cells are pseudo-replicates that cannot add independent evidence
about a between-patient contrast; only more patients can.

```{r bench-power, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Power to detect the planted niche effect. Left: rising with the number of patient samples. Right: flat in cells per sample under the mixed-effects correction."}
pp <- bench("power_points.csv")
if (!is.null(pp)) {
  pp$method <- factor(pp$method, levels = names(spide_pal$method))
  p_s <- ggplot(subset(pp, sweep == "samples"),
                aes(S, power_raw, colour = method)) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = "patient samples (S)", y = "power", title = "Power vs samples")
  p_n <- ggplot(subset(pp, sweep == "cells"),
                aes(n_per, power_raw, colour = method)) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = "cells per sample", y = NULL, title = "Power vs cells")
  p_s + p_n + patchwork::plot_layout(guides = "collect") &
    theme(legend.position = "bottom")
}
```

The fixed-effects mode looks the most powerful here, but that apparent power is
inflated by the same anti-conservatism that broke its null calibration above — a
power comparison is only fair once false-positive rates are matched, which the
next figure does.

## False discovery rate is controlled

At a reference design point we check that the **realised** false discovery
proportion (FDP) stays at or below the nominal FDR threshold, and how many true
positives are recovered (the true-positive rate, TPR) at each threshold.

```{r bench-fdr, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Left: realised FDP versus the nominal FDR threshold; points on or below the dashed y = x line mean FDR is controlled. Right: true-positive rate (recall) at each threshold."}
pf <- bench("power_fdr.csv")
if (!is.null(pf)) {
  ref <- subset(pf, sweep == "samples")
  ref <- aggregate(cbind(fdp, tpr) ~ fdr + method, ref, mean)
  ref$method <- factor(ref$method, levels = names(spide_pal$method))
  p_fdp <- ggplot(ref, aes(fdr, fdp, colour = method)) +
    geom_abline(slope = 1, intercept = 0, linetype = 2, colour = spide_pal$ref) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 2) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = "nominal FDR", y = "realised FDP", title = "FDR control")
  p_tpr <- ggplot(ref, aes(fdr, tpr, colour = method)) +
    geom_line(linewidth = 0.8) + geom_point(size = 2) + ylim(0, 1) +
    scale_colour_spide("method") +
    labs(x = "nominal FDR", y = "TPR (recall)", title = "Recall")
  p_fdp + p_tpr + patchwork::plot_layout(guides = "collect") &
    theme(legend.position = "bottom")
}
```

## Robustness to the spatial arrangement of cells

Real niches are not always smooth gradients. The study repeats the power
experiment under four spatial layouts: a **gradient**, patchy **clustered**
hotspots, a **random** arrangement with no spatial structure (a negative
control, where the niche effect should vanish), and a **multiniche** setting with
a second, competing structured cell type.

The four layouts differ in *where the niche cell type sits*, and therefore in the
shape of the planted signal. To make the simulation background concrete, we draw
one sample of each layout — just as in *The spatial niche and the planted signal*
above — and show two views: where each cell type sits, and the resulting planted
signal in the index cell type.

```{r layout-demo}
layouts <- c("gradient", "clustered", "random", "multiniche")

# one sample per layout: cell positions, cell types, and the planted signal
# (the niche-dependent effect on index cell A), mirroring the simulator
layout_demo <- function(layout, n = 900, field = 500, seed = 1) {
  set.seed(seed)
  x <- runif(n, 0, field)
  y <- runif(n, 0, field)
  bw <- 0.12 * field
  bumps <- function() {
    cen <- cbind(runif(4, 0, field), runif(4, 0, field))
    p <- rowSums(vapply(seq_len(nrow(cen)), function(k)
      exp(-((x - cen[k, 1])^2 + (y - cen[k, 2])^2) / (2 * bw^2)), numeric(n)))
    p / max(p)
  }
  pot <- switch(layout, gradient = x / field, random = rep(0.5, n),
                clustered = bumps(), multiniche = bumps())
  # niche (B) cells enrich where the potential is high; A and C fill the rest
  ct <- ifelse(runif(n) < pmin(0.85, 0.1 + 0.75 * pot), "B",
               sample(c("A", "C"), n, replace = TRUE))
  if (layout == "multiniche") {           # a second, competing structured niche
    swap <- ct == "C" & runif(n) < pmin(0.8, 0.1 + 0.7 * bumps())
    ct[swap] <- "D"
  }
  # planted effect lives in the index (A) cells, scaled by the niche potential
  data.frame(x = x, y = y, cell_type = ct, layout = layout,
             signal = ifelse(ct == "A", 2.5 * pot, NA_real_))
}
# a different seed per layout (so placements differ), keeping n at its default
demo <- do.call(rbind, Map(function(l, s) layout_demo(l, seed = s),
                           layouts, seq_along(layouts)))
demo$layout <- factor(demo$layout, levels = names(spide_pal$layout))
```

```{r layout-cells, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 3.1, fig.cap = "Cell-type layout for one sample of each spatial scenario. The niche cell type B is a smooth gradient, patchy hotspots, spatially unstructured, or accompanied by a second structured niche D, respectively."}
ggplot(demo, aes(x, y, colour = cell_type)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.6, alpha = 0.85) +
  facet_wrap(~ layout, nrow = 1) +
  scale_colour_spide("celltype", name = "cell type") +
  coord_equal() +
  guides(colour = guide_legend(override.aes = list(size = 2.5, alpha = 1))) +
  labs(title = "Cell-type layout by spatial scenario")
```

```{r layout-signal, fig.width = 9, fig.height = 3.1, fig.cap = "The planted signal in the index cell type (A) for each layout: the niche-dependent effect follows the local density of niche cells. It is a gradient, hotspots, and flat noise (the random negative control) respectively."}
ggplot(subset(demo, cell_type == "A"), aes(x, y, colour = signal)) +
  geom_point(size = 0.9) +
  facet_wrap(~ layout, nrow = 1) +
  scale_colour_viridis_c(option = "magma", name = "planted effect") +
  coord_equal() +
  labs(title = "Planted signal in index (A) cells")
```

The bars below turn this into numbers: recall at 5% FDR and the *niche
specificity* — how often the true niche cell type is identified as the strongest
association for a responding gene.

```{r bench-spatial, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Recall and niche specificity across spatial layouts. The niche effect is recovered under structured layouts (gradient, clustered, multiniche) and correctly collapses under the random negative control."}
sp <- bench("spatial.csv")
if (!is.null(sp)) {
  long <- rbind(
    data.frame(layout = sp$layout, metric = "recall @ 5% FDR",
               value = sp$recall_05),
    data.frame(layout = sp$layout, metric = "niche specificity",
               value = sp$niche_specificity))
  long$layout <- factor(long$layout, levels = names(spide_pal$layout))
  ggplot(long, aes(layout, value, fill = layout)) +
    geom_col(width = 0.7) +
    facet_wrap(~ metric) +
    scale_fill_spide("layout") +
    ylim(0, 1) +
    labs(x = NULL, y = NULL, title = "Robustness across niche layouts") +
    theme(legend.position = "none",
          axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 30, hjust = 1))
}
```

# Summary and reproducing this study

* The `spiDE` simulator reproduces the defining features of real spatial data:
  ten-thousand-plus genes, counts dominated by zeros and ones, gene-specific
  means and a downward mean–variance trend.
* Under this realistic model the **mixed-effects modes control the false-positive
  rate** where the fixed-effects mode does not, and they **control FDR** at the
  nominal level.
* **Statistical power is driven by the number of patient samples**, not the
  number of cells per sample — a direct consequence of correcting cell-level
  pseudo-replication.
* The niche effect is **recovered across spatial layouts** and correctly
  vanishes under a spatially random negative control.

The companion vignettes drill into two components: *Combining correlated niche
p-values* (the within-gene p-value combiner) and *Speeding up the mixed-effects
fit* (the PQL subsampling trade-off).

The complete study — the simulator, the scenario definitions, and SLURM scripts
for a compute cluster — is in the package's `research/` directory. To regenerate
the summary tables used above at full scale, run the study (see
`research/README.md`), then `Rscript research/R/install_results.R` and re-run
`vignettes/precompile.R`.

```{r session}
sessionInfo()
```
